TLEN.en = {
  'sec.muscle-growth.1.h': 'What actually changes',
  'sec.muscle-growth.1.b': 'Resistance training repeatedly challenges a muscle to produce force. Over weeks, muscle fibres can increase their contractile protein content, so the fibre cross-sectional area grows. This is hypertrophy. It is a slow, cumulative structural change — not something that appears after one hard session.',
  'sec.muscle-growth.2.h': 'A simplified model, clearly labelled',
  'sec.muscle-growth.2.b': 'A common teaching model has three steps: a training session creates a stimulus; muscle protein synthesis rises for roughly a day or two; if recovery and nutrition support it, a small amount of net protein is retained. Repeat that hundreds of times and the change becomes measurable. This model is useful and also simplified — real tissue-level work describes multiple interacting signals.',
  'sec.muscle-growth.3.h': 'Tension, and what is still debated',
  'sec.muscle-growth.3.b': 'Mechanical tension — force transmitted through the muscle — is treated as the central stimulus. Metabolic accumulation and exercise-induced damage are often discussed as additional contributors, but their independent contribution to long-term growth is less settled. Reviews of the sensing mechanisms stress that the picture is not a single pathway.',
  'sec.muscle-growth.4.h': 'Swelling is not growth',
  'sec.muscle-growth.4.b': 'After unaccustomed or very damaging training, water and inflammatory activity can make a muscle feel and measure larger for days. That is not new contractile tissue. This is why a trend over weeks is meaningful and a single reading is not.',
  'sec.muscle-growth.5.h': 'What ANCESTOR can and cannot tell you',
  'sec.muscle-growth.5.b': 'ANCESTOR tracks loads, repetitions, sets and effort across sessions. It does not measure muscle size. Any statement about "growth" in this app is an inference from performance trends, not a measurement of your tissue.',
  'sec.energy-fat.1.h': 'Energy balance, stated carefully',
  'sec.energy-fat.1.b': 'Body energy stores change when energy intake and energy expenditure differ over time. That statement is true and also less predictive than it sounds, because both sides adapt: appetite, spontaneous activity and metabolic rate all respond to sustained change.',
  'sec.energy-fat.2.h': 'The "7,700 kcal per kg" rule of thumb',
  'sec.energy-fat.2.b': 'You will see the claim that a 7,700 kcal deficit equals 1 kg of fat. Treat it as a rough starting point only. Adipose tissue is not pure lipid, and the body adapts during sustained weight change, so the real figure drifts as a person loses or gains. Models that account for this outperform fixed rules — which is why no calculator here will project your body composition.',
  'sec.energy-fat.3.h': 'Burning fat during a session is not the same as losing fat',
  'sec.energy-fat.3.b': 'The mix of fuel used during exercise depends on intensity, duration and recent intake. Exercising in a state that burns a higher proportion of fat does not by itself produce greater fat loss; what matters is the sustained balance over weeks.',
  'sec.energy-fat.4.h': 'Spot reduction is not supported',
  'sec.energy-fat.4.b': 'Training a muscle does not selectively remove the fat overlying it. Fat change happens systemically over time, and where it comes from first varies between people for reasons this app cannot assess.',
  'sec.energy-fat.5.h': 'What ANCESTOR does with this',
  'sec.energy-fat.5.b': 'ANCESTOR logs mechanical work — loads, reps and sets. It does not estimate calories burned, does not estimate body fat, and does not project body composition. If you see a number here, it is a logged training value, not a metabolic measurement.',
  'sec.strength-power.1.h': 'Strength is specific',
  'sec.strength-power.1.b': 'Strength is the capacity to produce force in a defined task. Get better at a movement and you are specifically better at that movement — some of it carries over, much of it does not. This is why the app compares you to your own history on the same exercise, never to another person.',
  'sec.strength-power.2.h': 'Early gains are largely neural',
  'sec.strength-power.2.b': 'In the first weeks of training, people often get stronger faster than the muscle visibly changes. Better coordination, motor-unit recruitment and reduced inhibition account for much of that early rise. Structural change accumulates more slowly on top of it.',
  'sec.strength-power.3.h': 'Load, reps and what each favours',
  'sec.strength-power.3.b': 'Heavier loads tend to produce larger gains in maximal-strength tests; a wide range of loads can produce similar hypertrophy when sets are taken close to failure. So the rep range in your plan follows your selected goal, not a belief that only one range "works".',
  'sec.strength-power.4.h': 'Estimated 1RM is an estimate',
  'sec.strength-power.4.b': 'ANCESTOR shows an estimated one-repetition maximum derived from your loads and reps. It is a modelling convenience for tracking trends, not a tested maximum. Do not use it to select a competition attempt.',
  'sec.movement-mechanics.1.h': 'Joints, levers and moment arms',
  'sec.movement-mechanics.1.b': 'A movement is produced by muscles pulling on bones across joints. The torque a muscle must produce depends on the load and on the moment arm — the perpendicular distance between the joint and the line of the load. Change your posture or range and you change the demand, which is why the same weight can feel different in a different position.',
  'sec.movement-mechanics.2.h': 'Primary and secondary involvement',
  'sec.movement-mechanics.2.b': 'Most exercises distribute work across several muscle groups. The muscle doing most of the work is the primary mover; others assist or stabilise. ANCESTOR assigns each exercise a set of muscle weights representing that distribution. These weights are a simplification used for volume accounting — they are not electromyography.',
  'sec.movement-mechanics.3.h': 'Range of motion is a training variable',
  'sec.movement-mechanics.3.b': 'Working through a larger range often means producing force at longer muscle lengths and in weaker joint positions. Reduced range can let you use more load. Neither is universally better; the choice depends on the goal, the joint and the person.',
  'sec.movement-mechanics.4.h': 'Technique is not verified here',
  'sec.movement-mechanics.4.b': 'ANCESTOR cannot see you. It records what you lifted and what you reported. It never claims to have assessed your technique, and progression rules treat "technique consistency" as your own judgement, not as a measured value.',
  'sec.cardio-health.1.h': 'More than one kind of fitness',
  'sec.cardio-health.1.b': 'Resistance training builds strength and muscle. Aerobic activity supports cardiovascular and metabolic health. Flexibility and neuromotor training support range and control. They are different qualities and they coexist — pursuing one does not make the others optional.',
  'sec.cardio-health.2.h': 'General activity guidance',
  'sec.cardio-health.2.b': 'Major position stands summarise weekly dose ranges for aerobic and resistance activity for apparently healthy adults. They are population-level starting points to be adapted to capacity and circumstance, not prescriptions for an individual with a medical condition.',
  'sec.cardio-health.3.h': 'What this app is not',
  'sec.cardio-health.3.b': 'ANCESTOR is not a medical device and gives no diagnosis. If you have a health condition, are pregnant, or have symptoms such as chest pain, unusual breathlessness or fainting, seek professional advice before changing your training.',
  'sec.sleep-recovery.1.h': 'Sleep is reported, not measured',
  'sec.sleep-recovery.1.b': 'If you log sleep, ANCESTOR uses the hours you entered and nothing else. There is no wearable connection and no physiological measurement. Everything downstream inherits that limitation.',
  'sec.sleep-recovery.2.h': 'Why the app asks when you sleep',
  'sec.sleep-recovery.2.b': 'Night-shift workers sleep during the day. An app that assumes sleep happens at night would mislabel their rest. ANCESTOR anchors the biological day on your usual wake time, so daytime sleep is handled as normal rather than as an error.',
  'sec.sleep-recovery.3.h': 'How the estimate is built',
  'sec.sleep-recovery.3.b': 'A per-muscle default is scaled by work performed, effort level, training style, experience, reported sleep, reported soreness and joint state, and performance trend. A bounded learned multiplier adjusts slowly from your own results. The output is an estimate with stated factors — visible wherever the number appears.',
  'sec.sleep-recovery.4.h': 'What it deliberately does not claim',
  'sec.sleep-recovery.4.b': 'No hormone levels, no diagnosis of overtraining, no injury detection, and no statement that a muscle has "recovered". Persistent pain, illness or unexplained performance loss warrant professional assessment, not a lower training load.',
  'sec.nutrition.1.h': 'Protein, in general terms',
  'sec.nutrition.1.b': 'Resistance training increases protein needs relative to sedentary guidelines. Meta-analytic work suggests benefits plateau for most people at intakes well above the minimum recommendation, with age and training status shifting the picture. These are general patterns, not a target computed for you.',
  'sec.nutrition.2.h': 'Timing is less dramatic than marketed',
  'sec.nutrition.2.b': 'The "anabolic window" is wider than supplement marketing implies. For most people total daily intake matters more than hitting a precise post-session minute.',
  'sec.nutrition.3.h': 'This app does not calculate your diet',
  'sec.nutrition.3.b': 'ANCESTOR does not track food, does not estimate your energy expenditure, and does not generate a nutrition prescription. If it ever discusses protein, it is education — not advice about your body.',
  'sec.aging-mobility.1.h': 'Capacity changes, trainability does not disappear',
  'sec.aging-mobility.1.b': 'Strength, power and balance tend to decline with age on average, but resistance training remains effective across the lifespan. What changes is the dose, the exercise selection and the recovery allowance — not whether training works.',
  'sec.aging-mobility.2.h': 'Function is a legitimate goal',
  'sec.aging-mobility.2.b': 'Getting out of a chair, carrying loads, climbing stairs and recovering from a stumble are trainable outcomes. ANCESTOR supports functional and mobility goals alongside hypertrophy for exactly this reason.',
  'sec.aging-mobility.3.h': 'Adaptation is individual, not diagnostic',
  'sec.aging-mobility.3.b': 'Seated options, wheelchair-compatible options, low-impact choices and mobility work are adaptations you choose. They are not a statement about your diagnosis, and no single adaptation suits everyone with the same label. Where clinical judgement is relevant, the app says so.',
  'sec.training-principles.1.h': 'Progressive overload, defined',
  'sec.training-principles.1.b': 'To keep adapting, the demand must increase over time. That increase can come from load, repetitions, sets, range of motion, control, or reduced rest — not only from adding plates. Progression is earned by performance, not granted by time elapsed.',
  'sec.training-principles.2.h': 'Why ANCESTOR will not add weight on a schedule',
  'sec.training-principles.2.b': 'A rule that says "two weeks have passed, add 2.5 kg" ignores whether you actually hit the target. ANCESTOR increases load only when every prescribed set reaches the top of the rep range at the intended effort. If the evidence is not there, the load holds; if performance declines twice in a row, it steps back.',
  'sec.training-principles.3.h': 'Volume is a variable with trade-offs',
  'sec.training-principles.3.b': 'Higher weekly set volumes are associated with greater average hypertrophy. ANCESTOR tracks weekly sets per muscle group so changes are visible and reviewable — it does not declare an optimum for you.',
  'sec.training-principles.4.h': 'Adherence dominates',
  'sec.training-principles.4.b': 'The best programme is the one sustained long enough to matter. This is why ANCESTOR treats missed sessions as information rather than failure, and why consistency is a selectable goal.',
  'sec.scientific-literacy.1.h': 'Not all evidence is equal',
  'sec.scientific-literacy.1.b': 'Systematic reviews and meta-analyses sit above individual trials for most practical questions; well-designed trials sit above observational work; mechanistic reasoning and expert opinion sit lower. Recency is not quality — a new study does not outrank a well-supported body of work.',
  'sec.scientific-literacy.2.h': 'Why single studies rarely change a recommendation',
  'sec.scientific-literacy.2.b': 'With flexible analyses, small samples and publication pressure, any one result can be fragile. ANCESTOR therefore requires a source to be reviewed and of usable quality before it can affect a user-facing rule. Nothing deploys straight from the literature.',
  'sec.scientific-literacy.3.h': 'AI is not a source',
  'sec.scientific-literacy.3.b': 'Explanations in this app are written to be traceable. Every research claim links to an evidence record with a title, authors, year and identifier where available. An AI-generated sentence is never treated as a citation.',
  'sec.scientific-literacy.4.h': 'Correlation, causation and you',
  'sec.scientific-literacy.4.b': 'Recovery estimates in ANCESTOR are associated with your logged history; they are not demonstrated causes of your next performance. The app separates observations from interpretations and says which is which.',
  'sec.misconceptions.1.h': 'A short tour of confident wrongness',
  'sec.misconceptions.1.b': 'Fitness culture is full of claims stated with total certainty and thin support: that a pump causes growth, that soreness proves effectiveness, that a narrow post-workout window decides your results, that exercising a muscle burns the fat on top of it, that a new study overturns decades of work. Each of these has a clear verdict in the evidence.',
  'sec.misconceptions.2.h': 'Why the myths persist',
  'sec.misconceptions.2.b': 'They are simple, they are actionable, and they are repeated by people with commercial incentives. A claim being popular is not evidence that it is true — and a supplement being legal is not evidence that it works.',
  'sec.misconceptions.3.h': 'The habit worth building',
  'sec.misconceptions.3.b': 'Ask what would change your mind. If a claim has no measurement attached, no comparison group and no mechanism, it is not a finding — it is a story.'
};

